---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Water Filtration Systems for Home",
    "url": "/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Water Test Kits",
    "url": "/best-water-test-kit/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Water Softener Systems",
    "url": "/best-water-softener-system/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Under-Sink Water Filters",
    "url": "/best-under-sink-water-filter/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Water Filters",
    "url": "/best-portable-water-filter/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-15", modified: "2026-11-15" });
const title = "7 Best Water Filters for Well Water in 2026: Test, Then Match";
const description =
  "The best water filter for well water is sized to your test results, not the most stages. Seven systems for sediment, iron, smell and bacteria, including an RO with UV.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ispring-wgb32bm",
    "name": "iSpring WGB32BM 3-Stage Whole House Water Filter System (Iron & Manganese)",
    "category": "Whole House, 3 Stages, Sediment + Carbon + Iron/Manganese, 20\" Big Blue",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Third-stage FM25B cartridge listed for iron up to 3 ppm and manganese up to 1 ppm",
      "5-micron sediment and carbon block stages ahead of the iron cartridge",
      "Listed at 15 GPM through 1-inch ports, enough for most well-fed homes",
      "20-inch Big Blue housings take widely available replacement cartridges",
      "Iron cartridge rated for about 50,000 gallons at 3 ppm iron, per the listing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not a disinfection system: it does nothing for bacteria",
      "Cartridge iron media is a poor fit for wells far above 3 ppm iron",
      "Three 20-inch housings need wall space and a solid mounting surface"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best all-round cartridge system for a typical well with sediment, mild iron and a little manganese.",
    "description": "The WGB32BM is the system most well owners should look at first, because it handles the three problems a typical private well actually has in one row of housings. Stage one is a 5-micron polypropylene sediment cartridge, stage two is a carbon block for taste and odor, and stage three is iSpring's FM25B iron and manganese reducing cartridge. The listing rates that third stage for iron up to 3.0 ppm and manganese up to 1.0 ppm, and gives about 50,000 gallons of life at the top of that iron range. That ppm ceiling is the spec that matters: if your test shows iron at 1 or 2 ppm, this system is sized for your water, and if it shows 6 ppm, it is not. The housings are the 4.5 x 20-inch Big Blue size, so replacement cartridges are easy to find, and the listing gives 15 GPM through 1-inch ports, which suits most homes with one well pump. The trade-off is that it is still a cartridge system. It does not disinfect, so a well with coliform needs UV or shock chlorination as well, and very high iron wears the iron cartridge out fast. If your iron reading is above 3 ppm, skip to the backwashing iron filter section below instead of buying cartridges you will replace every few months."
  },
  {
    "id": "home-master-hmf3sdgfec",
    "name": "Home Master HMF3SDGFEC Whole House Three Stage Water Filtration System (Fine Sediment, Iron and Carbon)",
    "category": "Whole House, 3 Stages, Radial Flow Iron, 1\" Ports",
    "badge": "Best for Iron",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Radial flow cartridges designed to keep flow up as media loads with iron",
      "Listing specifies iron, manganese and sulfur reduction up to 3 ppm combined",
      "Four-layer sediment stage graded from 25 down to 1 micron",
      "Listed at 15 GPM with 1-inch ports",
      "Operating pressure range of 20-95 PSI fits typical well pump settings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The 3 ppm limit is combined, so iron plus manganese plus sulfur must stay under it",
      "Proprietary cartridges cost more than generic Big Blue cartridges",
      "Still no bacteria treatment"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick when iron stains and a faint sulfur smell are the main complaints and you want to keep full water pressure.",
    "description": "Home Master built this system for well water specifically, and the detail that sets it apart is the radial flow design. Water passes through the cartridges from the side rather than end to end, which the maker says keeps flow steady as the media loads up with iron and sediment. That matters on a well, because a clogged iron cartridge shows up as weak showers long before it shows up as stained fixtures. Stage one is a four-layer fine sediment cartridge graded from 25 down to 1 micron, stage two is the iron reduction cartridge, and stage three is granular activated carbon for taste and odor. Retail listings give 15 GPM through 1-inch ports and a 20-95 PSI operating range, which suits most pressure-switch settings. Read the iron spec carefully: the listing says the system handles iron, manganese and hydrogen sulfide up to 3 ppm combined, not 3 ppm each. A well with 2 ppm iron and 1.5 ppm manganese is already over that line. The other trade-off is cartridge cost, since these are Home Master parts rather than generic sizes. If your combined reading sits under 3 ppm and you care most about pressure, this is the strongest cartridge option here."
  },
  {
    "id": "waterdrop-whf3t-fg",
    "name": "Waterdrop WHF3T-FG 3-Stage Whole House Water Filter System for Well Water (Iron & Manganese)",
    "category": "Whole House, 3 Stages, 5 Micron, Iron & Manganese Reduction",
    "badge": "Best Value Iron Filter",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Waterdrop states 95.9% iron and 99.7% manganese reduction in SGS testing",
      "Rated at 15 GPM maximum flow",
      "5-micron sediment rating with a grooved PP prefilter",
      "Materials certified to NSF/ANSI 372 for lead-free content",
      "Waterdrop gives 6-12 months of life for the iron and manganese cartridge"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Waterdrop gives percentage reductions, not a maximum ppm of incoming iron",
      "One-year warranty is shorter than some rivals",
      "Not a disinfection system"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A well-priced iron and manganese system for wells with modest readings, if you accept that the maker does not publish a ppm ceiling.",
    "description": "Waterdrop's WHF3T-FG is the value choice for a well with light iron and manganese. It uses three cartridges: a grooved polypropylene sediment filter, an iron and manganese reducing cartridge, and an activated carbon stage, with a 5-micron filtration rating across the system. Waterdrop states that third-party testing by SGS showed 95.9% iron reduction and 99.7% manganese reduction, and it rates the system for well water and municipal water at up to 15 GPM. The materials carry NSF/ANSI 372 certification for lead-free content, which covers what the parts are made of rather than what the filters remove. The one spec we would like to see and could not find is a maximum incoming iron level in ppm. A percentage reduction tells you how well it does on the test water, not how long the cartridge lasts on yours, so treat it as a system for modest readings, roughly the same range the iSpring and Home Master systems are rated for, and confirm with Waterdrop if your iron is higher. Waterdrop gives 6-12 months of life for the iron cartridge and a one-year warranty on the system. If you want a published ppm limit to plan around, the iSpring WGB32BM is the safer buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "express-water-heavy-metal-3-stage",
    "name": "Express Water Whole House Water Filter System, Heavy Metal, 3-Stage (KDF)",
    "category": "Whole House, 3 Stages, Sediment + KDF + Carbon Block, 1\" Ports",
    "badge": "Best for Metallic Taste",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "KDF stage of copper-zinc media aimed at heavy metals, plus sediment and carbon block",
      "Listed at 0.25 gallons per second, which works out to 15 GPM",
      "Standard 1-inch connections",
      "Express Water rates each stage at up to 100,000 gallons or 6-12 months",
      "Pressure gauges on the frame make clogged cartridges easy to spot"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No iron ppm limit published, so it is not a dedicated iron filter",
      "40-80 PSI operating range: check it against your pressure switch",
      "No bacteria treatment"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A solid three-stage system for wells with a metallic taste or mixed metals, once a test confirms iron is low.",
    "description": "The Express Water heavy metal system takes a different approach from the iron-specific picks above. Its middle stage is KDF, a copper and zinc media that Express Water lists for heavy metals along with chlorine, and it sits between a sediment cartridge and an activated carbon block. Express Water lists the system at 0.25 gallons per second, which is 15 GPM, through standard 1-inch connections, and rates each stage at up to 100,000 gallons or 6-12 months depending on water quality. Iron appears on its list of contaminants, but there is no ppm limit attached, so we would not buy it as a well's only iron treatment. It makes the most sense for a well where a test shows little iron but a metallic taste, or as the system downstream of a dedicated iron filter. Two practical notes for well owners. The listed operating range is 40-80 PSI, so a well pump set to 30/50 PSI will run below that window at the bottom of every cycle; check your pressure switch first. And the frame and gauges make the system heavy and wide, so plan the wall space. If iron is your main problem, choose the iSpring or Home Master system instead, and if arsenic is on your test report, look at a certified point-of-use reverse osmosis system for drinking water."
  },
  {
    "id": "ispring-wsp-50",
    "name": "iSpring WSP-50 Spin Down Sediment Filter, Whole House Prefilter, 50 Micron",
    "category": "Spin-Down Prefilter, 50 Micron Stainless Screen, 20 GPM",
    "badge": "Best Budget Prefilter",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Reusable 50-micron stainless steel screen that you flush instead of replace",
      "Listed at up to 20 GPM",
      "1-inch MNPT and 3/4-inch FNPT threads fit common supply lines",
      "Clear housing shows when sand has built up",
      "Protects the cartridges downstream so they last longer"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "50 microns stops sand and grit, not fine silt or iron",
      "20-90 PSI and 40-100°F limits must match your system",
      "Needs regular flushing or the screen clogs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to stop well sand from wrecking everything downstream. Put one in front of any cartridge system.",
    "description": "A spin-down filter is not a complete well water system, but it is the first thing we would install on almost any well, and the iSpring WSP-50 is the easy default. It is a clear housing around a reusable 50-micron stainless steel screen. Sand, grit and pipe scale catch on the screen and settle into the bottom of the housing, and you clear them by opening the flush valve for a few seconds rather than buying a new cartridge. iSpring lists it at up to 20 GPM with a 20-90 PSI pressure range and a 40-100°F temperature range, and it has 1-inch MNPT and 3/4-inch FNPT threads, which covers most well supply lines. The reason it matters is cost. A 5-micron sediment cartridge that has to catch sand by itself can clog in weeks on a sandy well, and so can the iron cartridge behind it. A spin-down in front takes the coarse load, so the cartridges only see fine particles. The limit is the screen size: 50 microns stops sand you can feel, not silt, clay or dissolved iron. If your well pumps a lot of sand at high flow, the larger Rusco below is the step up. Otherwise, pair this with any of the three-stage systems above."
  },
  {
    "id": "rusco-spin-down-1-12-100",
    "name": "Rusco Spin-Down Sediment Filter with 100 Mesh Polyester Screen (1-12-100-F)",
    "category": "Spin-Down Prefilter, 100 Mesh Polyester Screen",
    "badge": "Best for Sandy Wells",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "100 mesh polyester screen, which Rusco's mesh chart equates to roughly 152 microns",
      "Clear cover shows how much sediment has built up",
      "Bottom ball valve flushes sediment out without removing the screen",
      "Rusco sells replacement screens in many mesh sizes, coarse to fine",
      "Hand-tightened cover, so no tools are needed to service it"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Coarser than the iSpring WSP-50 screen, so it is a first stage only",
      "Rusco makes several body and port sizes, so check the size on the listing before you order",
      "Slip-style connections may need adapters for threaded pipe"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A long-established spin-down separator for wells that send a lot of sand, used as the first stage ahead of finer filters.",
    "description": "Rusco has made Spin-Down sediment filters for decades, and its screened separators are a common first stage on wells that send sand and grit into the house. This listing pairs a Rusco Spin-Down housing with a 100 mesh polyester screen, which Rusco's own mesh chart equates to roughly 152 microns. That is coarser than the iSpring WSP-50's 50-micron screen, and that is the point: a coarse first stage takes the bulk of the sand without restricting flow, and the cartridge stages downstream handle the fine material. The clear cover shows the sediment as it builds up, a ball valve at the bottom flushes it out, and the cover comes off by hand for a full cleaning; Rusco notes that using tools on the cover can void its warranty. Rusco also sells the same housings with finer and coarser screens, so you can change the mesh later without replacing the body. The one thing to check before ordering is size: Rusco makes Spin-Down bodies in several port sizes with different flow ratings, and the right one is the one that matches your main line and pump. Check the port size and flow rating on the listing against your pipe, or ask a plumber, before you buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "ispring-rcc7ak-uv",
    "name": "iSpring RCC7AK-UV 7-Stage Reverse Osmosis System with Alkaline and UV",
    "category": "Point-of-Use RO, 75 GPD, Alkaline Remineralization + UV Stage",
    "badge": "Best Drinking Water System",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "iSpring states certification to NSF/ANSI 58 for TDS reduction",
      "11-watt UV stage switched on and off by a flow sensor",
      "Alkaline remineralization stage after the RO membrane",
      "75 gallons per day of production, per iSpring",
      "Treats only the water you drink and cook with, so filters last longer"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Point of use only: it does not treat showers, laundry or fixtures",
      "Needs 45-70 PSI feed pressure; low well pressure may need a booster pump",
      "Untreated iron and hardness shorten membrane life, so pretreat first"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The drinking-water layer for a well: RO plus a UV stage at the kitchen tap, behind whatever whole-house treatment your test calls for.",
    "description": "The RCC7AK-UV is not a whole-house filter, and that is the point. Whole-house cartridges deal with sediment, iron and taste across every tap; this system deals with the water you drink, under the kitchen sink. It is a reverse osmosis system with five filtration stages, then an alkaline remineralization stage and an 11-watt UV stage that a flow sensor switches on when water runs. iSpring states certification to NSF/ANSI 58 for TDS reduction and lists 75 gallons per day of production, with a maximum feed TDS of 750 ppm. For a well, the UV stage is the reason to choose this model over the plain RCC7AK: it is the one pick here with a stage designed to treat microorganisms, at a single tap. That does not make it a fix for a well that tests positive for bacteria. The rest of the house still gets untreated water, so a positive result still means a call to your health department and treatment at the well or the main line. Two practical limits matter. iSpring lists 45-70 PSI feed pressure, and well systems that drop to 30 or 40 PSI may need a booster pump. And iron and hardness foul RO membranes, so put it behind sediment and iron treatment, and a softener if your water is hard."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Water Filters for Well Water",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-water-filter-for-well-water/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best water filter for well water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most wells with sediment and mild iron, the iSpring WGB32BM is the best water filter for well water on Amazon. It combines 5-micron sediment, carbon and an iron cartridge the listing rates for iron up to 3 ppm and manganese up to 1 ppm. Add a spin-down prefilter for sand, and UV or shock chlorination if your test shows bacteria."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do whole house filters remove bacteria from well water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Sediment, carbon, KDF and iron cartridges are not disinfection systems and should not be relied on for coliform or E. coli. Bacteria need UV treatment or shock chlorination. If your well tests positive, stop drinking it untreated and contact your local health department before you buy equipment, because the cause is often a damaged well cap or casing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know what filter my well needs?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Test first. A state-certified lab test for coliform, nitrate, pH, hardness, iron, manganese and TDS tells you exactly which treatments you need. Compare each number with the ppm limits filter makers publish. Sediment needs a spin-down and cartridge, iron needs iron media, hardness needs a softener, and bacteria need UV or chlorination."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a water filter remove iron from well water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, within limits. Iron-reduction cartridges such as the iSpring FM25B are listed for iron up to 3 ppm, and the Home Master system for up to 3 ppm of iron, manganese and sulfur combined. Above those levels, cartridges clog fast, and a backwashing iron filter installed by a water treatment dealer is the more practical choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where should a well water filter be installed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On the main line after the pressure tank and pressure switch, and before the water heater and any softener. Add shutoff valves on both sides and a bypass loop so you can change cartridges without cutting water to the house. If your pipes are metal, install a grounding jumper across the filter, and hire a plumber if you are unsure."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I change well water filters?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Makers typically quote 6-12 months per cartridge, or a gallon rating such as 50,000 or 100,000 gallons. On a well, sediment and iron levels set the real interval, so heavy sand or iron near the cartridge limit means more frequent changes. Pressure gauges before and after the housings show when a cartridge is clogging. Flush spin-down screens whenever sediment builds up."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a whole house filter reduce my water pressure?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Slightly, and more as cartridges load up. Choose a system rated at or above your pump's GPM, with ports matching your main line, usually 1 inch. Check the filter's operating range against your pressure switch: a 30/50 PSI setup falls below some systems' listed minimums. A clogged sediment cartridge is the most common cause of a sudden pressure drop."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "water filter for well water",
      "best water filter for well water",
      "well water filtration system",
      "whole house water filter for well water",
      "iron filter for well water",
      "spin down sediment filter",
      "well water treatment"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Water Filters for <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Well Water</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A private well has no utility testing it for you, so the best water filter for well water is the one sized to your test results, not the one with the most stages. Sediment, iron, smell or bacteria each need a different fix. Here are seven that cover them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ispring-wgb32bm"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Start With the Test
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#test-first" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Test Your Well Water First</a>
          <a href="#contaminants" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Each Problem Needs</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 7 Best Well Water Filters</a>
          <a href="#bacteria" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Filters and Bacteria</a>
          <a href="#whole-house-vs-pou" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Whole House vs Point of Use</a>
          <a href="#flow-rate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Flow Rate and Pressure</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Install</a>
          <a href="#costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Filter Change Costs</a>
          <a href="#high-iron" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Iron Above 3 ppm</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A private well has no utility testing it for you. The right filter depends on what your water actually contains, so this guide starts with the test, then matches each problem to the equipment that fixes it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="test-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Test Your Well Water First: The Filter Depends on the Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best water filter for well water is the one sized to your test results, not the one with the most stages. That is why every recommendation on this page starts with a test report.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Test For</h3>

          <p>
            At minimum, test for total coliform bacteria, nitrate, pH, hardness, iron, manganese and total dissolved solids. Add hydrogen sulfide if the water smells of rotten eggs, and arsenic, lead or uranium if your area is known for them; your county health department or state geological survey usually knows. The CDC recommends testing a private well at least once a year for coliform bacteria, nitrates, total dissolved solids and pH, and again after flooding, a repair, or any change in taste, color or smell.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lab Test or Home Kit?</h3>

          <p>
            Home test strips are a quick way to see whether iron, hardness or pH is a problem at all, and our guide to the <a href="/best-water-test-kit/">best water test kits</a> covers the ones worth buying. For bacteria and for anything you plan to spend real money on, use a state-certified laboratory. A lab report in ppm (milligrams per liter) is what you compare against the limits each filter maker lists below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="contaminants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Each Well Water Problem Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Well water problems come in a handful of types, and each has a matching fix. The mistake is buying one box and hoping it covers all of them. Match each line on your report to the treatment below, then put the treatments in order: sediment first, then iron, then softening, then disinfection, then drinking water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sediment and Sand: Spin-Down Plus a Cartridge</h3>

          <p>
            Sand, grit and silt are the most common well complaint and the cheapest to fix. A reusable spin-down screen such as the iSpring WSP-50 or the Rusco Spin-Down catches the coarse material and flushes clean with a valve. A 5-micron sediment cartridge behind it catches what gets through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Iron and Manganese: Iron-Reduction Media, Within Its Limit</h3>

          <p>
            Iron leaves orange stains and a metallic taste; manganese leaves black or brown stains. Both need dedicated iron-reduction media. Read the maker's limit in ppm: the iSpring WGB32BM's iron cartridge is listed for iron up to 3 ppm and manganese up to 1 ppm, and the Home Master listing specifies up to 3 ppm of iron, manganese and sulfur combined. Above those levels, cartridges clog quickly and a backwashing iron filter is the better tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rotten-Egg Smell: Hydrogen Sulfide</h3>

          <p>
            A sulfur smell usually means hydrogen sulfide gas. Carbon stages reduce light odor, and the Home Master system counts sulfur toward its 3 ppm combined limit. Heavy sulfide needs air injection or oxidation equipment from a water treatment dealer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hardness: That Is a Softener's Job</h3>

          <p>
            Calcium and magnesium cause scale, spotty dishes and short water heater life. None of the cartridge systems here soften water. If your report shows hardness above roughly 7 grains per gallon (about 120 ppm), add a softener after the iron stage; our guide to the <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">best water softener systems</a> covers sizing by grain capacity. For a well specifically, our <a href="/best-water-softener-for-well-water/">water softener for well water</a> guide covers the iron limits a softener can tolerate, and if you only want to curb scale without salt, a <a href="/best-water-conditioner/">water conditioner</a> is the lighter option, though it leaves the hardness in the water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bacteria and Coliform: UV or Shock Chlorination</h3>

          <p>
            Coliform bacteria need disinfection, not filtration. That means a UV system or shock chlorination of the well, and a positive result means calling your local health department first. The table below shows which pick covers which problem.
          </p>

