        <div id="bacteria" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Do Well Water Filters Remove Bacteria? Not Without a UV Stage
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most important section on the page. Sediment, carbon, KDF and iron cartridges are not disinfection systems. Unless a system includes a UV stage or another disinfection method its maker certifies, assume it does nothing for coliform or E. coli.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If Your Test Is Positive for Coliform or E. coli</h3>

          <p>
            Stop drinking the water untreated, use bottled water or boil water for drinking and cooking, and contact your local or county health department. They can tell you how to disinfect the well, help find the cause, which is often a cracked well cap, a failed casing seal or surface water running toward the wellhead, and tell you when to retest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shock Chlorination</h3>

          <p>
            Shock chlorination means adding a measured dose of chlorine to the well and plumbing, letting it sit, then flushing it out. Health departments commonly recommend it after flooding, repairs or a single positive test, followed by a retest. Follow your health department's instructions for the dose, and never shock-chlorinate a system with an RO membrane or carbon filters in line; bypass them first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UV Systems for Ongoing Protection</h3>

          <p>
            If bacteria keep coming back, a whole-house UV system after the sediment and iron stages is the usual long-term answer. UV only works on clear water: iron, manganese, hardness and sediment shield bacteria from the light and coat the quartz sleeve, which is one more reason the sediment and iron stages come first. For drinking water alone, the iSpring RCC7AK-UV adds a UV stage at one tap, but it does not protect showers or other faucets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-house-vs-pou" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Whole House vs Point of Use: Most Wells Need Both
          </h2>

          <p>
            Well water treatment works in layers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-House Systems</h3>

          <p>
            A whole-house system sits on the main line after the pressure tank and treats every gallon: toilets, laundry, showers and the water heater. This is where sediment, iron, manganese and hardness belong, because those cause stains and wear everywhere. The iSpring, Home Master, Waterdrop and Express Water systems are all whole-house units, and our pillar guide to the <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration systems for home</a> covers whole-house options for city water too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Point-of-Use Systems</h3>

          <p>
            A point-of-use system treats one tap, usually the kitchen sink. Reverse osmosis is the most thorough option for dissolved contaminants such as nitrate, arsenic and TDS, which whole-house cartridges do not touch. If your report flags any of those, an under-sink system is the right tool; see our guide to the <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">best under-sink water filters</a> for options without UV.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How They Fit Together</h3>

          <p>
            A full well setup typically runs: spin-down, sediment and iron cartridges, softener if the water is hard, UV if bacteria are a risk, then RO at the kitchen sink. For power outages or trips away from home, a <a href="/best-portable-water-filter/">portable water filter</a> is a useful backup, and if you rely on a generator, check <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">what size generator runs a well pump</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flow-rate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Flow Rate and Pressure Drop: Size the Filter to Your Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a city supply, a filter that restricts flow is an annoyance. On a well, it can be a real problem, because your water pressure already cycles between the pump's cut-in and cut-out settings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gallons Per Minute</h3>

          <p>
            Most homes peak somewhere between 6 and 12 GPM when a shower, a washing machine and a faucet run together. The cartridge systems here are listed at 15 GPM and the iSpring spin-down at 20 GPM, which leaves headroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Port Size Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Match the filter's ports to your main line. Most well homes run 1-inch or 3/4-inch pipe, and port sizes vary by model and sometimes by listing, so check each system's port size on its listing before you order. Reducing a 1-inch line to 3/4-inch fittings to fit a cheaper housing costs you flow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pressure Drop and Your Pressure Switch</h3>

          <p>
            Many well pumps run on a 30/50 or 40/60 PSI pressure switch. Check that the bottom of your range sits inside each filter's listed operating range: the Express Water system lists 40-80 PSI and the iSpring RO lists 45-70 PSI, so a 30/50 setup sits below both at the bottom of each cycle. Pressure gauges before and after the filters show when a cartridge is due, long before stains do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Install a Well Water Filter Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installing a whole-house filter is a manageable job for a confident DIYer, but it involves cutting into the main line and, on many homes, the electrical ground. If any step below is unfamiliar, hire a licensed plumber; the cost is small next to a leak on the main line.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Install After the Pressure Tank</h3>

          <p>
            Filters go on the main line after the pressure tank and pressure switch, and before the water heater and softener. That way the pump and tank still see the well directly, while everything in the house sees filtered water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Add Shutoff Valves and a Bypass</h3>

          <p>
            Put a shutoff valve on each side of the filter and a bypass loop with its own valve around it. With a bypass, you can change cartridges, flush a spin-down or shock-chlorinate the well without shutting off water to the whole house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Bridge the Ground If Your Pipes Are Metal</h3>

          <p>
            Many older homes use the metal water pipe as part of the electrical grounding path. Plastic filter housings break that path. If your plumbing is copper or galvanized steel, install a grounding jumper cable with clamps on the pipe either side of the filter. If you are unsure whether your system relies on the pipe for grounding, ask an electrician.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Filter Change Costs and Schedule
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase price is the smaller part of what a well filter costs. Compare the replacement cost of each system before you buy it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sediment, Carbon and Iron Cartridges</h3>

          <p>
            Makers quote cartridge life of 6-12 months or a gallon figure, such as 50,000 gallons for the iSpring FM25B at 3 ppm iron and up to 100,000 gallons per stage for Express Water. On well water, sediment and iron levels decide the real interval. Iron near the top of a cartridge's limit means changes at the short end of the range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RO Filters, Membranes and UV Lamps</h3>

          <p>
            Under-sink RO systems have prefilters to change on a schedule, a membrane that lasts longer, and on UV models a lamp with its own replacement interval.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="high-iron" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Iron Above 3 ppm: When Cartridges Are Not Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every iron cartridge on this list is sized for modest iron. If your test shows well above 3 ppm, or iron plus manganese above the combined limit, cartridges become an expensive treadmill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Backwashing Iron Filters</h3>

          <p>
            Tank-style iron filters use air injection or oxidizing media to turn dissolved iron into particles, trap them in a media bed, then backwash themselves on a timer. They handle much higher iron levels than cartridges and need little routine attention beyond the backwash cycle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can a Softener Remove Iron?</h3>

          <p>
            Softeners can remove small amounts of dissolved ferrous iron along with hardness, and many softener makers list an iron limit. They are not designed for oxidized iron or for high levels, which foul the resin. If your water is both hard and iron-rich, treat iron first and soften after it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Filter Before Testing the Well</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a lab report you are guessing. A system rated for 3 ppm iron on a well with 7 ppm will clog in weeks, and nothing in a cartridge kit fixes bacteria. Test first, then buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Cartridge Filter as Disinfection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sediment, carbon and iron cartridges do not disinfect. If your test shows coliform, call your health department and plan for UV or shock chlorination rather than trusting a carbon stage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Spin-Down on a Sandy Well</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sand clogs 5-micron cartridges fast. A reusable spin-down screen in front costs little and can stretch cartridge life a long way on wells that pump grit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Combined ppm Limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some makers rate iron, manganese and sulfur together. Two readings that each look safe can add up to more than the limit, so total them before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing Without a Bypass or Ground Jumper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No bypass means no water while you service the filter, and plastic housings on metal pipe can break the electrical ground path. Add both, or have a plumber do it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

