              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best water filter for well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most wells with sediment and mild iron, the iSpring WGB32BM is the best water filter for well water on Amazon. It combines 5-micron sediment, carbon and an iron cartridge the listing rates for iron up to 3 ppm and manganese up to 1 ppm. Add a spin-down prefilter for sand, and UV or shock chlorination if your test shows bacteria.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do whole house filters remove bacteria from well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Sediment, carbon, KDF and iron cartridges are not disinfection systems and should not be relied on for coliform or E. coli. Bacteria need UV treatment or shock chlorination. If your well tests positive, stop drinking it untreated and contact your local health department before you buy equipment, because the cause is often a damaged well cap or casing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know what filter my well needs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Test first. A state-certified lab test for coliform, nitrate, pH, hardness, iron, manganese and TDS tells you exactly which treatments you need. Compare each number with the ppm limits filter makers publish. Sediment needs a spin-down and cartridge, iron needs iron media, hardness needs a softener, and bacteria need UV or chlorination.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a water filter remove iron from well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, within limits. Iron-reduction cartridges such as the iSpring FM25B are listed for iron up to 3 ppm, and the Home Master system for up to 3 ppm of iron, manganese and sulfur combined. Above those levels, cartridges clog fast, and a backwashing iron filter installed by a water treatment dealer is the more practical choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should a well water filter be installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the main line after the pressure tank and pressure switch, and before the water heater and any softener. Add shutoff valves on both sides and a bypass loop so you can change cartridges without cutting water to the house. If your pipes are metal, install a grounding jumper across the filter, and hire a plumber if you are unsure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I change well water filters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Makers typically quote 6-12 months per cartridge, or a gallon rating such as 50,000 or 100,000 gallons. On a well, sediment and iron levels set the real interval, so heavy sand or iron near the cartridge limit means more frequent changes. Pressure gauges before and after the housings show when a cartridge is clogging. Flush spin-down screens whenever sediment builds up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a whole house filter reduce my water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slightly, and more as cartridges load up. Choose a system rated at or above your pump's GPM, with ports matching your main line, usually 1 inch. Check the filter's operating range against your pressure switch: a 30/50 PSI setup falls below some systems' listed minimums. A clogged sediment cartridge is the most common cause of a sudden pressure drop.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
